        What can the test suite not see?

            python3 kit/mutate.d/mutate.py --list         what would be tried
            python3 kit/mutate.d/mutate.py                the run
            python3 kit/mutate.d/mutate.py --target text  one target

        A suite that passes proves nothing by itself: it passes on the code it
        was written against. Change the code, and if it still passes, that
        change is invisible to it -- and so is the bug that would look the
        same. This makes those changes one at a time and reports what nothing
        noticed. A survivor is a gap in the tests, not a bug in the code.

        Counts are not coverage. 732223 checks agreeing is what the suite says
        about itself; this is what somebody else says about the suite.

        Three outcomes, never two:

            caught     the lane failed, which is what should happen
            survived   the lane passed, so nothing can tell the difference
            invalid    it did not build, or it did not stop

        Folding invalid into either of the others ruins it: into survived and
        the list drowns in noise, into caught and real gaps disappear.

        Each mutation is named by the routine it is in and a hash of the text
        around it, never by a line number, because these files move. That is
        what lets ledger.json hold a verdict on a survivor that has been
        looked at -- some genuinely change nothing -- and lets the next run
        separate the ones already judged from the ones nobody has seen.

        Each target names the lane that would have to notice it. Mutating
        text.c and running verify proves nothing, so the pairing is written
        down in TARGETS rather than assumed.

        The operators, in the order they earn their keep:

            plusminus   a number, one either way. Both real bugs found in this
                        library were this shape: a restart one label out and a
                        flag one byte out.
            target      where a branch goes, rather than whether it is taken.
                        A condition flip cannot produce the string_find bug;
                        this can.
            branch      the sense of a branch, and C relational operators
            swar        one of the three SWAR constants for another. Plus one
                        on those means nothing -- every byte carries a bit
                        pattern the algorithm rests on -- so they are swapped
                        instead. The strrchr bug was exactly this shape.

        Calibrated against both bugs this library actually had: the swar
        operator over string_last_of_or_end is caught 12 of 12, and the
        string_find restart is caught by the cases in verify.c written for it.
        A harness that cannot find a bug you have already proved is a broken
        harness, so that check comes first.

        What this does not tell you. It measures whether the tests can detect
        changes to code that is here. It says nothing about behaviour never
        written -- a coreutils flag not implemented, an exit status never
        emitted. That is the other half of being 1:1 with the system's tools
        and it needs a different probe. A clean run here is not that claim.
